{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 909a3e3fddebcae7eab7c7fd74f779ecd18538c6, resting on all 59 submitted artifacts, packages/room/README.md first.\n\nThe five 8faa2ef9 findings are addressed for newly created state: the pending cohort stores its exact expected commit before a remote write and checks that commit/parent on reopen; workspace readiness rechecks the lease deadline and seals overdue expiry; monotonic attention positions deliver later items at an existing head and wake subscriptions; objections seal reopening effects from the shared calculator; versioned transactional migrations preserve attention and pending workspaces and supply conservative historical evidence facts. \"*\" grants are now fixed at grant time, agreeing with lane L. The 13 amendment 2 lane A edits are represented and tested. Two independent workerd/SQLite checks expose remaining upgrade and authorization failures.\n\n1. P2 — upgrade existing pending-publication metadata along with its schema (core.ts PendingPublication/pendingPublication/publish; store.ts migrations). The old persisted cohort has parent, through, hash, checkpoint and retained digests, but no expected field. It is still decoded directly as the new interface. No migration adds the expected commit. After upgrade an exact lost-response commit no longer passes the ref fence; if the ref is still at the parent, the confirmed result cannot equal undefined either. Both paths can wedge publication indefinitely.\n\nI published a valid cohort with its push/read-back replies lost, removed only the new expected field to match the previous persisted format, dropped schema_version to exercise upgrade, and evicted the DO. The existing migrations reopen successfully, but two successive publishLog calls return unavailable; publishedThrough remains -1 while the exact own pushed commit remains on the remote. The diagnostic changes no checkpoint, retained evidence or signed entries.\n\nAdd a versioned migration or explicit guarded conversion of the old pending cohort, deriving and persisting its exact commit from its recorded parent, entries, checkpoint and retained bytes with the publisher's serialization before further writes. If a legacy serialization needs compatibility, make that explicit; do not infer authorship from matching entry lines, discard the pending cohort or silently start a fresh publication. Test upgrade with the old pending cohort both before push and after an applied push whose answer was lost, including repeat reopen and foreign-writer rejection.\n\n2. P2 — authorize first import binding with a fresh clock, not the request-start value (founding.ts foundRoom/checkGrant; registry.ts bind). foundRoom receives now once, awaits first-admin signature verification and reg.byRepo, then checkGrant verifies asynchronously and compares notAfter with the earlier now. A grant that has already expired during those awaits still authorizes the first binding, where the retry exception does not apply.\n\nI drafted an import with a valid configured-operator grant expiring in one second, then injected two seconds of clock advancement during the registry lookup. There was no prior binding. foundRoom successfully bound and founded the room and read its repository after the grant's notAfter. This is the real founding code, real registry and real Room SQLite, with an injected clock/lookup delay; no live service was used. R-GEN-12 requires notAfter not to have passed unless that exact repository is already bound to this room as a retry.\n\nRead the current deployment clock after asynchronous checks and enforce the verified grant deadline at the atomic first-binding boundary. Carry the validated deadline into the registry's bind command if needed to close the final RPC delay, while retaining the explicitly allowed completion-forward retry of an identical existing binding after expiry. Add expiry during signature/lookup/bind delay cases, equality at expiry, and the existing after-binding retry as a positive case.\n\nArchitecture: the exact-commit fence is the right publication boundary, and monotonic attention positions fix the causal-delivery gap. Versioned storage needs to include durable work payloads as well as columns; those payloads are part of the protocol that survives deployment and host loss. Founding's repository authorization and immutable binding are sound improvements, but their expiry decision needs to agree with the actual binding time.\n\nSecurity: the new repository identity, configured-operator signature and one-registry binding protect against unauthorized repository selection and duplicate sequencers. A first binding with an expired grant remains possible; a completed identical binding's expiry exception must not widen to a first binding. The migration finding is an availability failure, not evidence that a foreign commit can now be accepted.\n\nSimplification: retain the single publisher serialization, one obligation calculator and shared migration runner. Give persisted cohorts an explicit version/conversion instead of treating a TypeScript interface as a storage migration. Keep the fresh authorization boundary in one registry operation rather than spreading stale time snapshots through the founding path.\n\nValidation: clean install (196 packages), root workspace typecheck, 66/66 Node tests and 207/207 workerd tests passed. Two independent workerd/real-SQLite diagnostics reproduced the upgrade wedge and expired first-import binding, 2/2 passed after correcting a diagnostic's hex import. All 59 artifact paths were compared with previous reviewed head a5a3406a: 30 identical blobs including package-lock.json, all 29 changed/new artifacts reviewed. Approved main amendment 2 contract changes were checked against that ratified scope. Diagnostics are only in /tmp/artroom-checker-room-A-v3/packages/room/test/workerd/checker-v3-review.test.ts; candidate source was not changed. No deployment or live Artifacts operation was run.\n\nP1.9 remains open exactly as the candidate declares: phase 2b must connect the real lane B landing engine and lane L publisher (including commitFor), the real Artifacts adapter and repository-identity mapping, and joint shared-SQLite acceptance tests covering check carry, filtered inputs, authority replay and the full contract matrix. This is required completion work, not a demand to claim those adapters already exist. The passing stand-in suite and amendment 2 tests do not waive it. Schedule estimates do not reduce scope.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c4a1eb7e5bf410c268d06929c28a7eeb76f8e404","binding":"assigned","head":"909a3e3fddebcae7eab7c7fd74f779ecd18538c6","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9f92e5a6bcde71d2571bfc4785ccb1df6fecd757\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d2409eaede0361d81de52cb482c667967a64779c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e518cef01950fe9a14927151af6273e34d5efe41","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}